  "summary": "Four preprints pair large-scale sequence generation or ranking with yeast assays or fitness-landscape benchmarks, making model predictions checkable.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational generation or ranking of protein and enzyme sequences with outcomes that can be checked. The activation-domain study generates 500,000 candidates, tests 18,750 in yeast, and introduces MADRIGAL, a consensus ML predictor. The splicease study combines AlphaFold3 with Rosetta to rank millions of variants and map the substrate scope of HypX and PlpX. AlphaEnzyme is a structure-grounded foundation model for catalyst retrieval, Km and mutational effects, benchmarked and validated. The VAE study is the most methodological: it benchmarks VAEs against a training-free information-theoretic method for rare, highly functional sequences on two fitness landscapes. What is new is the pairing of large-scale generation or ranking with explicit experimental or benchmark validation, and the VAE comparison questions whether trained generative models are necessary.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
